.\" Manual page for fm84. View with: man ./doc/fm84.1
.TH FM84 1 "2026-10-30" "fm84 0.39.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fm84 \- a synthwave dual\-pane TUI file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fm84
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ LEFT
.RI [ RIGHT ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fm84
is a two\-panel file manager for the terminal, in the line of Norton
Commander and Midnight Commander.
Each panel lists a directory, with tabs of its own; files are copied and
moved from the active panel to the other one.
It has a built\-in viewer with syntax highlighting, hex and image views, a
built\-in editor, find files by name and content, and directory sizes counted
in the background.
.PP
The top border names the user and host fm84 runs as, reversed for root.
Over each panel is its directory, and the git branch it is on when it is in
a repository, read from the repository's files rather than by running git.
A click on the branch lists the repository's local branches, to switch to
another with Enter or a second click on it; that runs
.BR git (1),
2.23 or newer.
.PP
Anything that touches the disk is done off the thread that draws the screen,
so a network mount that has stopped answering never freezes the interface;
Esc stops waiting on it.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I LEFT
Directory for the left panel.
.TP
.I RIGHT
Directory for the right panel.
.PP
A directory given takes the place of the tab that would have shown; the
panel's other tabs stay.
Without one, a panel opens where it was when fm84 last quit if
.B Remember directories
is on (F11), and in the current directory otherwise.
A directory that does not exist is reported before anything starts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-choosedir " FILE"
On quitting, write the active panel's directory to
.IR FILE ,
for a shell function to
.B cd
to.
The name is written as it is, with nothing after it.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell\-init " SHELL"
Print
.BR cd84 ,
a shell function that starts fm84 with
.B \-\-choosedir
and then goes to that directory, and exit.
.I SHELL
is
.BR bash ", " zsh ", " fish
or
.BR powershell .
See
.BR "LEAVING THE SHELL WHERE FM84 WAS LEFT" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help, with where the config file is, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the options, for a directory whose name starts with a dash.
.SH ARCHIVES
Enter on a
.IR .zip ,
.IR .7z ,
.IR .tar ,
.IR .tar.gz ,
.IR .tar.zst ,
.I .tar.xz
or
.I .tar.bz2
(and
.IR .tgz ,
.IR .tzst ,
.IR .txz ,
.IR .tbz2 )
opens it in the panel like a directory, and its directories open as any do.
The panel's path runs through it, as
.IR ~/photos.zip/2024 ,
so tabs, history, bookmarks, Ctrl+L and remembered directories work inside
one; Backspace at its top comes back out onto the archive.
.PP
An archive is read-only.
F5 copies out of it, a directory whole, asking about files in the way as any
copy does; times and permissions come out as the archive kept them, and links
as links.
Alt+F9, or Ctrl+F9, extracts the whole of the archive under the cursor, or of
the one the panel is in, into the other panel or wherever its dialog is told.
Shift+F3 compares a file inside an archive with one on the disk or in another
archive, copying each out first.
F3 copies a file out to a scratch directory and shows it there; Enter and
Ctrl+O open it from there with the system's program.
F12 and the lines under the panels read entries in place, and Space and
Alt+Shift+Enter size directories from what the archive says.
Everything that would write into an archive says so and changes nothing:
renaming, making, deleting, moving, copying or linking into it, Alt+A and F4.
Find files does not reach inside one.
.PP
Alt+P packs what is chosen into a new
.IR .zip ,
.IR .7z ,
.IR .tar.gz ,
.IR .tar.xz ,
.I .tar.bz2
or
.IR .tar ,
as a job like a copy, keeping modes, times and links.
A zip or a 7z cannot hold a name that is not UTF-8, which is asked about; a
tar keeps it.
A 7z is packed solid.
The archive is written beside where it goes and put there whole, so a cancel
leaves nothing, and one made inside the directory being packed leaves itself
out.
One already at the name is written over only when the options say to
overwrite.
A
.I .tar.zst
is read but not made.
.PP
A compressed tar is read on past the end of its first stream, so files joined
end to end are one archive.
A 7z is listed from its header without unpacking anything; one packed solid
unpacks what comes before a file to reach it.
.PP
A name that would land outside where it is copied to, with
.BR .. ,
an absolute path or a drive, is left out of the listing, as is anything
that would be written through a link in the archive.
An encrypted zip entry is marked in the detail lines, and cannot be copied
out without the password, which fm84 does not ask for.
Nothing in an encrypted 7z can be copied out either, and one whose names
are encrypted too cannot be gone into.
.PP
An archive written while a panel is in it is read again; one deleted is
left.
The scratch directory is removed when fm84 quits.
.SH LEAVING THE SHELL WHERE FM84 WAS LEFT
A program cannot change the directory of the shell that started it, so
quitting fm84 leaves the shell where it was.
.B cd84
does the rest: it runs fm84, and on quitting goes to the directory of the
active panel, passing on its arguments and its exit status.
It stays put when fm84 did not quit normally, or quit with Shift+F10.
To have it, add one line to the shell's startup file:
.TP
bash, in ~/.bashrc
.B eval \(dq$(fm84 \-\-shell\-init bash)\(dq
.TP
zsh, in ~/.zshrc
.B eval \(dq$(fm84 \-\-shell\-init zsh)\(dq
.TP
fish, in ~/.config/fish/config.fish
.B fm84 \-\-shell\-init fish | source
.TP
PowerShell, in $PROFILE
.B fm84 \-\-shell\-init powershell | Out\-String | Invoke\-Expression
.PP
The function is printed by the fm84 it comes with, so it keeps up with it.
.SH KEYS
F1 inside fm84 lists these too.
.SS Panels
.TP 18
.B Up Down Left Right
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B Tab
Switch panels.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the directory, go into an archive, or open the file as the system
does.
See
.BR ARCHIVES .
.TP
.B Shift+Enter Ctrl+O
Open what is under the cursor as the system does, with the program it opens
that kind of file with; an archive too, which Enter goes into.
Ctrl+O is for terminals that send Shift+Enter as plain Enter; only those with
kitty's keyboard protocol, and Windows, tell the two apart.
.TP
.B Backspace
Go to the parent directory.
.TP
.B Home End
First or last entry.
.TP
.B PageUp PageDown
A page up or down.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Left Ctrl+Right
Open the directory under the cursor in the left or right panel.
.TP
.B Ctrl+U
Swap the panels, each with its tabs, cursor, selection and history;
the focus goes with the one it was in.
.TP
.B Alt+I
Send the other panel to the active one's directory.
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Edit the active panel's path in place, in the line above it, and go there
with Enter; Esc, or a click anywhere else, leaves it as it was.
A click on either panel's path does the same, in that panel.
.B Tab
fills out a name as far as the entries agree;
.B ~
is the home directory, and a path not starting with
.B /
is taken from the panel.
A file is landed on, in the directory it is in.
.TP
.B Alt+Left Alt+Right
Back and forward through the directories the tab has been in, landing on
the entry the cursor was on.
Each tab keeps its own.
.TP
.B Ctrl+D
Bookmarks.
.B Enter
or
.BR 1 ... 9
goes to one,
.B A
or
.B Insert
keeps the panel's directory,
.B D
or
.B Delete
forgets the one highlighted, and
.B Shift+Up Shift+Down
move it in the list.
.TP
.I letters and digits
Quick search: jump to the first name starting so, either case; Up and Down
go between the names that do.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the search; close a dialog, the viewer or the editor; stop counting
directory sizes; stop waiting on a directory that has not answered.
.TP
.B Space Insert
Select or deselect the entry.
Space on a directory also counts its size, unless it is a link to one.
.TP
.B Alt+Shift+Enter Alt+Enter
Count the size of every directory in the panel, a few at a time, the rest
shown waiting.
Links to directories are left out.
As
.BR du (1)
counts: a file with several hard links once, and nothing on
.IR /proc ,
.I /sys
and the like.
.TP
.B + \-
Select or deselect by pattern:
.B *
any run of characters,
.B ?
any one,
.B [abc]
and
.B [!a\-z]
classes, several separated by
.BR ; .
A pattern ending in
.B /
is for directories.
All in lower case matches either case.
.TP
.B *
Invert the selection of files.
.TP
.B Alt+*
Invert the selection of files and directories.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S
Filter the panel: only what the pattern matches is shown, narrowing as it
is typed.
A word without \fB*\fR, \fB?\fR or \fB[\fR is looked for anywhere in the
name; a pattern takes directories as well as files, and one ending in
.B /
directories only.
Enter keeps it, shown in the path bar; Esc puts back the filter there was;
an empty one shows everything.
What is selected but filtered out is let go.
The filter stays while the panel is in its directory, and goes when it
moves elsewhere.
.TP
.B Alt+H
Work out the checksums of what is chosen, a directory's files all the way
down, as a job: SHA-256, SHA-512 and MD5 at once, in one reading of each
file.
Tab or Right shows the next algorithm's, Shift+Tab or Left the one before.
C copies them \- one file's checksum alone \- and Enter writes them as
.BR sha256sum (1)
does, to
.I name.sha256
for one file or
.I SHA256SUMS
for several.
On a checksum file, written by
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR md5sum (1)
or in BSD's style, check the files it names instead, the failures listed
first.
.TP
.B Alt+A
Change the permissions, owner, group and modified time of what is selected,
or of the entry under the cursor.
A field left as it opened, or emptied, changes nothing.
The mode is octal, such as
.BR 755 ,
or as
.BR chmod (1)
takes it, such as
.BR u+x,go\-w ;
owner and group by name or number; the time as
.BR "2026\-10\-23 14:02:11" .
Tab, or a click on a field, moves between them.
Only the time on Windows.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C Alt+C
Copy the full paths, or the names, of what is selected, or of the entry
under the cursor, to the clipboard, a line each; on
.BR .. ,
the panel's own directory.
Sent over OSC 52, which the terminal has to allow.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F3 Ctrl+F4 Ctrl+F5 Ctrl+F6
Sort both panels by name, extension, date or size;
the same key again reverses the order.
A click on a column heading does the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Reload both panels.
.TP
.B Ctrl+T Ctrl+W
New tab, close tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+PgUp Ctrl+PgDn
Previous or next tab.
.TP
.B Alt+1 ... Alt+9
Go to tab 1 to 8, or the last.
.TP
.B Alt+F1 Alt+F2
Choose a drive for the left or right panel.
Ctrl works too.
.SS Function keys
The ones the screen answers are listed along the bottom border;
a click on one presses it.
.TP 18
.B F1
Help: the keys, then
.BR About ,
with the version, licence and where fm84 lives.
Like every popup it sits over the panels and is no taller than they are;
when it is longer, the arrows, PageUp and PageDown, Home and End and the
mouse wheel scroll it.
.TP
.B F2
Rename.
.TP
.B Shift+F2
Compare the panels' directories, as in Total Commander, and select in each
what differs from the other: an entry only it has, and of a file both have,
the newer, or both when the times agree and the sizes do not.
Times within two seconds count as the same, for FAT.
Directories both have are not gone into.
What was selected before is replaced, and the status bar says how many
differ.
.TP
.B F3
View the file.
.TP
.B = Shift+F3
Compare two files side by side: two selected in the active panel, or else
the file under the cursor against the one of the same name in the other
panel, or failing that the one under the cursor there.
Between the two,
.B |
marks a changed line,
.B <
one only on the left and
.B >
one only on the right.
F3 or N goes to the next difference, Shift+F3 or P to the previous; Esc
closes it.
Files that are the same, differ only in line endings, or are not text are
reported in the status bar instead.
Shift+F3 needs a terminal with kitty's keyboard protocol, such as kitty, foot,
WezTerm, Ghostty or Alacritty; elsewhere it cannot be told from a report of
the cursor's position, and
.B =
is the key.
The same holds for Ctrl+F3 and for Shift+F3 in the viewer.
.TP
.B F4
Edit the file, in the built\-in editor or the one set under F11.
.TP
.B F5
Copy to the other panel, or wherever the dialog is told.
A single file can be given a new name on the way.
.TP
.B Alt+F5 Ctrl+Shift+F5
Make a symbolic link to what is chosen, asking where as F5 does: for one
entry, its name in the other panel; for several, the other panel's
directory, with a link in it for each.
Each points at the absolute path of what it links to, and nothing already
there is written over.
Tab makes hard links instead: files only, on the same filesystem.
Ctrl+Shift+F5 is as in Total Commander; kitty keeps it for reloading its
config, so Alt+F5 is there too.
.TP
.B F6
Move, as F5 copies.
.TP
.B F7
Create a directory.
.TP
.B Shift+F4
Create an empty file.
.TP
.B F8 Delete
Move to the trash, or delete if F11 says so.
.TP
.B Shift+F8 Shift+Delete
Delete for good.
.TP
.B F9
Open a terminal in the panel's directory.
.TP
.B Alt+F9 Ctrl+F9
Extract the whole of the archive under the cursor, or of the one the panel is
in, to the other panel or wherever the dialog is told.
.TP
.B Alt+P
Pack what is chosen into a new archive, asking where: on the other panel's
directory, named after what is packed.
Tab goes through the formats by the ending of the name.
.TP
.B F10
Quit.
Twice while a copy, move or delete runs; asks first about unsaved edits.
.TP
.B Shift+F10
Quit, leaving
.B cd84
in the directory it started from.
.TP
.B F11
Options.
.TP
.B F12
Preview the file under the cursor in the other panel.
.TP
.B Alt+F7 Ctrl+F7
Find files by name and, if asked, by content, down from the active panel's
directory.
.SS During a copy, move or delete
.TP 18
.B Esc
Cancel it.
.TP
.B R S A
Retry, Skip, or Skip all, when an entry fails.
.TP
.B Y N K A S B O
Overwrite, Skip, Keep both, Overwrite all, Skip all, Keep both all, or
Overwrite all older, when a file is already there.
Keep both leaves it, and writes the new one beside it as
.IR "name (2).ext" ,
numbered on past any already taken.
.TP
.B D
Delete for good, when the trash will not take an entry.
.SS Viewer
.TP 18
.B X
Hex view on or off; for an image, cycle Image, Text and Hex.
.TP
.B W
Wrap long lines, or scroll them sideways.
.TP
.B F
Fit or fill an image.
.TP
.B + \-
Zoom an image in or out.
.TP
.B PageUp PageDown
Previous or next picture in the folder, when one is open.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Copy the selection.
.SS Editor
.TP 18
.B Shift+arrows
Select text.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Select all.
.TP
.B Ctrl+X Ctrl+C Ctrl+V
Cut, copy, paste.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Undo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Y Ctrl+Shift+Z
Redo.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R F4
Replace; then
.BR Y ,
.B N
or
.B A
to replace, skip or replace all.
.TP
.B O R
Overwrite or reload, when the file changed on disk since it was opened.
.SS Viewer and editor
.TP 18
.B Ctrl+F
Find.
All in lower case matches either case.
.TP
.B F3 Shift+F3
Next or previous match.
.TP
.B Ctrl+G
Go to line.
.PP
Scroll bars are drawn in the frame's border, down the right and along the
bottom, when there is more than fits.
A click on the track scrolls a page that way; the thumb can be dragged.
In the editor they move the view and leave the cursor where it is.
.SH OPTIONS DIALOG
F11 opens the options, changed in place with Enter, Space, Left or Right and
saved as they change.
Among them: hidden files, sort order, columns, date format and size units;
confirmations, delete to trash, what to do when a destination exists, the
terminal and editor commands, remembering directories; the theme, icons,
clock, and the cursor's shape and whether it blinks; tab width, line numbers,
what the scroll bars are drawn with, highlighting and image settings.
The list scrolls within the panels when it does not fit, with Up and Down or
the mouse wheel.
.PP
The cursor, in fields and in the editor, is the terminal's own: a bar, a
block or an underline.
.B Classic
draws a reversed cell instead, for a terminal that cannot change the shape of
its cursor.
.PP
In the
.B Terminal
and
.B Editor
commands,
.B {}
stands for the directory or the file.
An editor without
.B {}
gets the file last.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/config
The options, as plain
.I key = value
lines, fine to edit by hand.
.I ~/.config/fm84/config
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set, and
.I %APPDATA%\efm84\econfig
on Windows.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/session
The panels' directories and tabs, kept when
.B Remember directories
is on.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fm84/bookmarks
The directories kept with
.BR Ctrl+D ,
a
.I bookmark = path
line each, in order.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config directory is; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B HOME
The config directory when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set, and where a typed
.B ~
leads.
.TP
.B TERMINAL
The terminal F9 opens when none is set under F11.
Without either, fm84 tries
.BR xdg\-terminal\-emulator ,
.BR alacritty ,
.BR kitty ,
.BR foot ,
.BR gnome\-terminal ,
.BR konsole ,
.B xfce4\-terminal
and
.BR xterm ,
in that order.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the trash is, as the freedesktop.org trash specification has it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Quit normally.
.TP
.B 1
Not run from a terminal, or an error that stopped it, writing the
.B \-\-choosedir
file among them.
.TP
.B 2
A mistake on the command line, such as an unknown option, a directory that
does not exist or a shell
.B \-\-shell\-init
has no
.B cd84
for.
.SH NOTES
Icons need a Nerd Font; F11 can switch them to plain letters.
Images are drawn as ASCII art in colour, and look best in a terminal with
true colour.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR mv (1)
.PP
